  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14108272/assets/126abe41ac185c082d8cc28628e2db4b2708dd84ba351718184d949ddf582d3c.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f$ is a surjection\n- $f$ is an injection\n- $f$ is a bijection",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- a surjection if $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- an injection if $\u2200\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider $y=2.$ As we can see from the diagram, there are no elements contained within $X$ that have an arrow to $2\\in Y.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $Y,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $X$ such that $f(x)=y.$ As a result, $f$ is *not* a surjection.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $f(1)=f(4)$ but $1\\ne 4.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ As a result, $f$ is *not* an injection.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement I is false (as well as statement II), $f$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"",
